==Old English==

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|gem-pro|ang}} {{m|gem-pro|*kattōn}}. Cognate with {{etyl|ofs|-}} {{term|katte|lang=ofs}}, {{etyl|osx|-}} {{term|katta|lang=osx}}, {{etyl|dum|-}} {{term|katte|lang=dum}} ({{etyl|nl|-}} {{term|kat|lang=nl}}), {{etyl|goh|-}} {{term|kazza|lang=goh}} ({{etyl|de|-}} [[Katze]]), {{etyl|non|-}} {{term|ketta|lang=non}} ({{etyl|sv|-}} {{term|katta|lang=sv}}). The word existed in the Germanic languages in a masculine gender also, represented in Old English by {{term|catt|lang=ang}}. The word appears to be related to {{etyl|LL.|-}} {{term|cattus|lang=la}} as well as to similar words in the Slavic and Celtic languages, but the ultimate source is uncertain.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|/ˈkɑtːe/|lang=ang}}

